#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

const std::vector<int> times = {930, 945, 1000, 1000, 1030, 1600};

// Should count the trades with start <= time < end, but checks time <= end.
int countInWindowOld(int start, int end) {
    int count = 0;
    for (int t : times) {
        if (t >= start && t <= end) {
            ++count;
        }
    }
    return count;
}

// The fix: the end of the window is not part of it.
int countInWindow(int start, int end) {
    int count = 0;
    for (int t : times) {
        if (t >= start && t < end) {
            ++count;
        }
    }
    return count;
}

int main() {
    // Each case: start, end, the expected count.
    const std::vector<std::vector<int>> cases = {
        {940, 1010, 3},
        {1100, 1200, 0},
        {930, 1000, 2},   // a trade exactly at the end
        {1000, 1000, 0},  // an empty window
        {1600, 1700, 1},  // the last trade exactly at the start
    };
    int oldFailed = 0;
    int newFailed = 0;
    for (const auto& c : cases) {
        int start = c[0], end = c[1], want = c[2];
        if (countInWindowOld(start, end) != want) {
            ++oldFailed;
            std::cout << "old countInWindow(" << start << ", " << end << ") = "
                      << countInWindowOld(start, end) << ", expected " << want << '\n';
        }
        if (countInWindow(start, end) != want) {
            ++newFailed;
            std::cout << "new countInWindow(" << start << ", " << end << ") = "
                      << countInWindow(start, end) << ", expected " << want << '\n';
        }
    }
    std::cout << "old: " << oldFailed << " of " << cases.size() << " failed\n";
    std::cout << "new: " << newFailed << " of " << cases.size() << " failed\n";
    return newFailed == 0 ? 0 : 1;
}

steps.body:
*Building Projects: Flags, Make, and CMake* put a `tests/` folder beside `src/` and `include/` and ran its test program, with its `check` and `near` helpers, through `ctest`, which counts a test as passed when it exits with 0. A real project has hundreds of tests, so each one is a function whose name says what it checks, and a failure report reads like a sentence. This part's example builds that shape by hand. Frameworks give it to you.

**GoogleTest**, Google's framework and the most common one in industry, provides the macros and the `main`:

```cpp norun
#include <gtest/gtest.h>
#include "quote.h"

TEST(Spread, IsAskMinusBid) {
    EXPECT_EQ(spreadTicks(100, 102), 2);
}

TEST(Spread, LockedMarketIsZero) {
    ASSERT_EQ(spreadTicks(100, 100), 0);
}
```

`EXPECT_EQ` records a failure and keeps going, `ASSERT_EQ` also ends the current test, and `EXPECT_NEAR(a, b, 1e-9)` is the framework's `near`. For each test, GoogleTest prints a `RUN` line as it starts and an `OK` or `FAILED` line as it ends, the file, the line, and both values of every failed check, and a count of passed and failed tests at the end. **Catch2** reads more like plain C++: `REQUIRE(spreadTicks(100, 102) == 2);` inside `TEST_CASE("spread is ask minus bid")`, and it still shows both sides on a failure. Neither depends on `NDEBUG`, so both check Release builds too.

Neither comes with the compiler, and the sandbox has neither. Install GoogleTest with `brew install googletest` on a Mac or `apt install libgtest-dev` on Ubuntu, and the test lines of that lesson's `CMakeLists.txt` become:

```text
find_package(GTest REQUIRED)
enable_testing()
add_executable(test_quote tests/test_quote.cpp)
target_link_libraries(test_quote PRIVATE quotes_core GTest::gtest_main)
include(GoogleTest)
gtest_discover_tests(test_quote)
```

`gtest_discover_tests` takes the place of `add_test` and registers every `TEST` on its own, so `ctest --test-dir build` reports each one. On Windows, Visual Studio's Test Explorer runs the same tests.

The habit matters more than the tool. Run the tests before every commit, start every bug fix with a failing test, and keep tests deterministic, with no clocks or random numbers in a checked result, the same rule the Check button follows. Three tools show what the tests miss. Code coverage, from `gcov` with g++ or `llvm-cov` with clang, marks every line no test ran. A fuzzer such as libFuzzer feeds a parser millions of generated inputs, usually with the sanitizers of *Debugging: Crashes, Sanitizers, and a Debugger* turned on, so any crash becomes a failing test. And CI runs the whole suite on every push, so a change that breaks a test never reaches the main branch.

example.code:
#include <iostream>

int g_failures = 0;
int g_tests = 0;
int g_failedTests = 0;

#define CHECK_EQ(actual, expected)                                   \
    do {                                                             \
        auto got = (actual);                                         \
        auto want = (expected);                                      \
        if (got != want) {                                           \
            ++g_failures;                                            \
            std::cout << "  line " << __LINE__ << ": " << #actual    \
                      << " is " << got << ", expected " << want << '\n'; \
        }                                                            \
    } while (0)

// Runs one test function and reports it by name, as test frameworks do.
#define RUN_TEST(test)                                                \
    do {                                                              \
        int before = g_failures;                                      \
        test();                                                       \
        ++g_tests;                                                    \
        if (g_failures == before) {                                   \
            std::cout << "[ OK   ] " << #test << '\n';                \
        } else {                                                      \
            ++g_failedTests;                                          \
            std::cout << "[ FAIL ] " << #test << '\n';                \
        }                                                             \
    } while (0)

// The code under test. In a project it lives in src/, the tests in tests/.
int spreadTicks(int bid, int ask) {
    return ask - bid;
}

int midTicks(int bid, int ask) {
    return bid + (ask - bid) / 2;
}

void spread_is_ask_minus_bid() {
    CHECK_EQ(spreadTicks(100, 102), 2);
    CHECK_EQ(spreadTicks(99, 100), 1);
}

void spread_of_locked_market_is_zero() {
    CHECK_EQ(spreadTicks(100, 100), 0);
}

void mid_rounds_down_between_ticks() {
    CHECK_EQ(midTicks(100, 101), 100);
    CHECK_EQ(midTicks(-3, -2), -3);
}

int main() {
    RUN_TEST(spread_is_ask_minus_bid);
    RUN_TEST(spread_of_locked_market_is_zero);
    RUN_TEST(mid_rounds_down_between_ticks);
    std::cout << g_tests << " tests, " << g_failedTests << " failed\n";
    return g_failedTests == 0 ? 0 : 1;
}

